      "raw": "<div>We played our first game yesterday and I&#39;ve been trying some more to sort out the timing questions that arose</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>Looking at the FAQ I believe there are definitely two principles</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>1) simultaneous effects are actually resolved in order (of the players choosing)</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>2) If an effect is triggered, it is fully resolved before continuing with those simultaneous effects (and can affect/amend/cancel those effects)</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>so are the following correct</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>a card effect attacks all hero targets</div><div>The players select the first hero target</div><div>By selecting that hero target, an effect/power destroys the card causing the attack</div><div>all the remaining attacks are cancelled</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>a card effect attacks all hero targets for 2 damage and another card gives a bonus 1 to the attack for a total of 3 damage</div><div>The players select the first hero target</div><div>By selecting that hero target, an effect/power destroys the card causing the bonus 1</div><div>all the remaining attacks are value 2 (even though &quot;simultaneous&quot;)</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>However, my main concern (because the other players didn&#39;t feel it was correct) is the following</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>Unity&#39;s&nbsp;Cryo Bot receives damage that would destroy it</div><div>However, when damage is dealt to it, it deals damage to non-hero targets</div><div>Does being destroyed, prevent the possible triggers on the card. (In our game we found this is important for Cryo Bot and Lady Luck)</div><div>I emailed Mike Booth (Spiffworld) and he seemed to think that being destroyed would prevent the triggers but it didn&#39;t sit well with the group yesterday.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>(we were playing with more complex characters as I had received Shattered Timelines and the mini-expansions but the base game has failed to arrive as yet ! &nbsp;However, the questions remain valid for us)</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>Thanks</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>Ian</div>",
